{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 48, :comments ["<b>Elishama son of Ammihud.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanchuma): Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korḥah would interpret the names: ״אֱלִישָׁמָע בֶּן עַמִּיהוּד״ — אֵלַי שָׁמַע, 'to me he listened,' and to her mistress he did not listen; עִמִּי הָיָה הוֹדוֹ, 'with me was his splendor,' and not with the wicked ones — end quote. It appears that the verse intends, in the phrase <b>eli shama</b>, that the reason Joseph gave his explanation was not on account of any physical impulse that would arise from the act, but rather out of fear of HaShem, as he said (Genesis 39:9): ״וְחָטָאתִי לֵאלֹהִים״, and this is the meaning of <b>elai shama</b>. It further intends, according to their words where they said (Sotah 36b) that the image of his father appeared to him, etc. — and it is well known that this is an appearance of the Shekhinah, for his father in his place knew nothing, even if he was still alive; rather, it was the Mighty One of Jacob, the Elohim of Israel, and this is the meaning of <b>elai shama</b>. And he doubled to say further <b>Ammihud</b>, to allude to what the verse states (ibid.): ״וְלֹא שָׁמַע אֵלֶיהָ לִשְׁכַּב אֶצְלָהּ לִהְיוֹת עִמָּהּ״ — meaning, ״לִשְׁכַּב״ etc. at its simple sense; ״לִהְיוֹת עִמָּהּ״ — she demanded of him closeness of his flesh to her flesh and he refused, and this is the meaning of ״עִמִּי הוֹדוֹ״ etc. And our Rabbis of blessed memory who said ״עִם רְשָׁעִים״ in the plural — perhaps their intention was to say that he did not guard himself from the wife of Potiphar alone, but from several like her."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The comment reads Elishama's name as an allusive acronym of phrases describing Joseph's resistance to Potiphar's wife; all bolded lemmata are the name-fragments being interpreted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:47.305941135Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}